 "intro_zh": "塞巴斯蒂亚是巴勒斯坦纳布卢斯省的一个村庄，位于纳布卢斯西北约 12 公里，被视为约旦河西岸最古老的持续有人居住地之一。公元前 9 世纪，这里被称为撒马利亚，曾是北以色列王国的都城；罗马时期由希律大帝扩建并改名为塞巴斯蒂亚。基督教和伊斯兰教传统均将这里视为施洗者约翰的埋葬地。",
 "intro_zh_source": "curated",
 "intro_en": "Sebastia (Arabic: سبسطية, Sabastiyah; Greek: Σεβαστή, Σεβάστεια, romanized: Sevasti, Sevasteia; Hebrew: סבסטיה, Sebastiya; Latin: Sebaste) is a Palestinian village of about 3,205 inhabitants, located in the Nablus Governorate of the State of Palestine, some 12 kilometers northwest of the city of Nablus. Sebastia is believed to be one of the oldest continuously inhabited places in the West Bank. In the 9th century BCE, it was known as Samaria, and served as the capital city of the northern Kingdom of Israel until it was destroyed by the Neo-Assyrian Empire around 720 BCE. It became an administrative center under Assyrian, Babylonian and Persian rule. During the early Roman period, the city was expanded and fortified by Herod the Great, who renamed it Sebastia in honor of emperor Augustus. Since the middle of the 4th century, the town has been identified by Christians and Muslims as the burial site of John the Baptist, whose purported grave is today part of Nabi Yahya Mosque. Conquered by Muslims in the 7th century, the present-day village of Sebastia is home to a number of important archaeological sites.",
 "intro_en_source": "wikipedia",
 "intro_local": "سبسطية هي بلدة فلسطينية تقع في محافظة نابلس على بُعد 12 كم شمال غرب مدينة نابلس. كما تُعتبر البلدة ذات تاريخ عريق وحضارة زاهرة امتدت لأكثر من 3000 عام، تخللها العديد من الأحداث والوقائع التاريخية الهامة لفلسطين وللمنطقة بشكل عام وأطلق عليها المؤرخون لقب عاصمة الرومان في فلسطين.",
 "intro_local_source": "wikipedia",
